  m_Name: AxialFlowCompressorData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: 'Axial Flow Compressor '
  engineDescription: An Axial Flow Compressor is a continuous-flow turbomachinery
    device used to increase the pressure of a gas by accelerating and progressively
    compressing it in a direction parallel to the axis of rotation. Its main components
    include the rotor, compressor blades, stator blades, casing, shaft, inlet guide
    vanes, bearings, and outlet diffuser. During operation, air enters the compressor
    axially and passes through successive stages consisting of rotating rotor blades
    and stationary stator blades. The rotor blades increase the velocity and energy
    of the airflow, while the stator blades convert part of this velocity into pressure
    and redirect the flow toward the next stage. Repeated compression through multiple
    stages produces a significant increase in air pressure before the compressed
    air reaches the outlet. Axial flow compressors are widely used in gas turbines,
    jet engines, power plants, and large industrial compression systems. From an
    engineering perspective, understanding blade aerodynamics, pressure ratio, stage
    efficiency, flow velocity, compressor surge, stall, blade loading, vibration,
    and temperature management is essential for achieving efficient and stable operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
